Recovered **all 5,986 points directly from the original ZIP**, and verified an exact match with the coordinates used in our simulation. No algorithm was rerun.

[Manhattan_Pickups_2015-01-15_0800-0815.json](sandbox:/workspace/scratch/289abe85887d/Manhattan_Pickups_2015-01-15_0800-0815.json) contains ordinary `[x,y]` coordinate pairs, the encoded integer coordinates, timestamps and metadata, **in original order**.

- **Date/source:** 15 January 2015, Manhattan yellow-taxi pickups, according to the archive.
- **Window:** seconds `0 ≤ t < 900` after 08:00; exactly 5,986 records.
- **Coordinates:** north-up planar coordinates in **kilometres**.
- **Exact simulator transformation:** `x = qx / 65535 * 8.8103`, `y = qy / 65535 * 18.8542`.
- The archive does **not** specify the geographic origin, lat/lon projection formula or timezone explicitly.
- It cites [OhadEitan/Capacity-Constrained-KKmeans](https://github.com/OhadEitan/Capacity-Constrained-KKmeans), directory `simulation/artifact/`.

Below is the **complete coordinate data**, losslessly packed to fit in the response. This textual version changes only point order: points are spatially sorted, and **all duplicates are retained**. It reconstructs the exact quantized coordinates used by the simulator.

Copy the JSON into `D`, then run this dependency-free JavaScript:

```javascript
const s = D.chunks["1"] + D.chunks["2"] + D.chunks["3"];
const alphabet =
  "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789+/";

const bytes = [];
let buffer = 0, bits = 0;
for (const ch of s) {
  if (ch === "=") break;
  buffer = (buffer << 6) | alphabet.indexOf(ch);
  bits += 6;
  if (bits >= 8) {
    bits -= 8;
    bytes.push((buffer >>> bits) & 255);
  }
}

const points = []; // Exact [x,y] coordinates, in kilometres.
let code = 0, delta = 0, multiplier = 1;
for (const b of bytes) {
  delta += (b & 127) * multiplier;
  if (b & 128) {
    multiplier *= 128;
    continue;
  }
  code += delta;
  delta = 0;
  multiplier = 1;
  let qx = 0, qy = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < 16; i++) {
    qx += ((code >>> (2 * i)) & 1) * 2 ** i;
    qy += ((code >>> (2 * i + 1)) & 1) * 2 ** i;
  }
  points.push([qx / 65535 * D.W, qy / 65535 * D.H]);
}
if (points.length !== 5986) throw Error("Incomplete coordinate data");

// To obtain ordinary compact JSON:
// JSON.stringify(points)
```

The three numbered chunks form one Base64 payload. Its decoded-byte SHA-256 is `2735e880733734c7579bbb8ebe53b569747545d78fadae4333ff841f2a325592`.

